SpringCard CCID TCP IFD Handler
===============================

This package provides the source code for a SpringCard TCP CCID IFD Handler for
pcsc-lite.

It is not a USB CCID IFD Handler. The supported readers expose a CCID-compatible
smart-card interface, so the upper PC/SC and CCID command handling remains close
to a USB CCID driver, but the transport is network-based and is very different
from USB hotplug.

The driver is derived from Ludovic Rousseau's CCID driver. SpringCard removed
the USB and serial reader management from that code base and added the network
transport used by SpringCard readers.


Authors of the original CCID-based library:
===========================================

- Ludovic Rousseau <ludovic.rousseau@free.fr>
- Carlos Prados for the PPS and ATR parsing code, taken from his towitoko
  driver in the towitoko/ directory.
- Olaf Kirch for the T=1 TPDU code, from the OpenCT package in the openct/
  directory.


Authors of SpringCard CCID TCP:
===============================

- Matthieu Barreteau <support@springcard.com>
- Sylvain ALBERT <support@springcard.com>


Supported readers:
==================

This driver is intended for SpringCard network readers and modules that support
CCID over TCP, including:

- E663 module
- SpringPark-SD
- TwistyWriter-IP
- E519 module
- SpringPark EVO

The reader must be reachable from the host running pcscd, and the pcsc-lite
reader configuration must point to the reader IP address and TCP port.


Supported operating systems:
============================

The driver targets Unix-like systems supported by pcsc-lite. It has primarily
been used on GNU/Linux systems.

macOS/Darwin support may require platform-specific packaging and validation.


Installation:
=============

See INSTALL for build, installation, pcsc-lite configuration and test
instructions.


Runtime behavior:
=================

The driver is loaded by pcscd through the pcsc-lite IFD Handler mechanism. Each
network reader is declared in the pcsc-lite reader configuration, typically
/etc/reader.conf or the directory returned by:

    pkg-config libpcsclite --variable=serialconfdir

The DEVICENAME field contains the network endpoint and connection options. See
INSTALL for examples.


Debug information:
==================

The driver uses the debug facility provided by pcscd. If pcscd logs to stdout
for a foreground debug session, the driver logs there as well. If pcscd logs to
syslog or the system journal, the driver follows that behavior.

The debug level may be set with the LIBCCID_ifdLogLevel environment variable
before starting pcscd. It is a bit mask:

- 1: critical errors
- 2: informational messages
- 4: communication dumps
- 8: periodic card-presence checks

The default level is 3, which enables critical and informational messages.
Restart pcscd after changing the environment or configuration used by the
driver.


Reader availability hook:
=========================

The installation includes a hook script named springcard-ccid-tcp-hook. The
driver calls this script asynchronously when a configured reader becomes
available or unavailable.

This hook is intended for sites where the network can be unreliable, such as
indoor or outdoor parking facilities. Integrators can use it to notify a
monitoring tool, alert an operator or trigger controlled recovery actions, for
example power-cycling network equipment through a supervised system.

The hook must remain small, secure and non-blocking from an operational point of
view. See INSTALL for its location, arguments and execution constraints.


